<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11614"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:embersageblue@aol.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Embersage<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Embersage<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, March 15, 2005 - 7:04 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->found this post <BR> <BR> <BR>I see there has not been a message posted here for quite a long time. Not sure if anyone even reads this anymore, but I hope Mr. Smit does. This has probably been noticed before but the acronym used at the bottom of the ransom letter S.B.T.C. Could stand for Southern Baptist of Texas Convention, I looked to see if they had any connections with Boulder &#40;although most large cities have Baptist connections&#41; and there are two churches that have a connection. They are Cornerstone of Boulder Valley and East Boulder Baptist Church. Just another conspiracy theory but perhaps the person who committed the crime had some affiliation with one of these churches &#40;maybe not a member, but could have been&#41;. He or she could have gotten that acronym from a church pamphlet or something like that. A long shot I know, but....  <BR> <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
